    <h1>Disclaimer</h1>
    <p><em>Last updated: 2026-10-18 · Applies to incenseherbs.com and data.incenseherbs.com</em></p>

    <blockquote>
      LanDeng publishes botanical incense knowledge — materials, techniques, history, and industry
      practice — for informational and educational purposes. It is not medical, legal, or investment
      advice, and it is not a substitute for professional guidance.
    </blockquote>

    <h2>1. Informational content, not medical advice</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing on this site is intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease or condition.
      Where materials are described in traditional or historical terms (for example, classical Chinese
      materia-medica language such as 行气止痛 or 温中), that is <strong>traditional knowledge</strong>,
      recorded as history and culture — <strong>not modern clinical evidence</strong>. Traditional uses
      are never presented as proven health effects.
    </p>
    <p>
      Incense is a fragrance and ritual practice. It is not a treatment, a sleep aid, or an anxiety
      remedy. If you have a medical concern, consult a qualified healthcare professional. Do not rely
      on this content to make health decisions, and do not consume any botanical material based on
      traditional-medicine descriptions without professional guidance.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Traditional use vs modern evidence</h2>
    <p>
      LanDeng separates traditional knowledge from scientific evidence. A historical record is reported
      as history; a scientific finding is reported as science, with its source and evidence tier; a trade
      term is labeled as trade terminology; and an uncertain claim is marked as uncertain. This separation
      is described in our <a href="/blog/landeng-editorial-methodology/">editorial methodology</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Responsible use and safety</h2>
    <p>
      Burning incense produces smoke and particulate matter. Burn in a well-ventilated space, avoid
      inhaling concentrated smoke directly, use a heat-safe holder, never leave burning incense
      unattended, and keep it away from children, pets, and flammable materials. Some materials carry
      stronger safety cautions — always read the material's own entry. "Natural" or "botanical" does
      not mean automatically safe. See our <a href="/safety/">safety page</a> for the full disclosure.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Commercial information</h2>
